  "hints": [
    "Live collaboration needs the server to push other people's changes. Which component does that?",
    "Concurrent edits to the same section need coordination. In this simulator that's a lock service; real editors use OT or CRDTs.",
    "Client \u2192 CDN, and Client \u2192 WebSocket Gateway (2) \u2192 Document Service (6) \u2192 Cache (2) + Redis Lock (2) + NoSQL (2)."
  ],
  "scoring": {
    "weights": {
      "performance": 0.35,
      "reliability": 0.3,
      "cost": 0.15,
      "architecture": 0.2
    },
    "latencyTargetMs": 45,
    "costBudget": 1400
  },
  "explanation": [
    "Each open document lives on one document server, and every client is connected to it over a WebSocket, so edits are broadcast instantly.",
    "Real editors resolve conflicts with Operational Transformation (Google Docs) or CRDTs (Figma, many newer editors). Both let concurrent edits merge instead of overwrite.",
    "The lock here stands in for that coordination: without it, two writers silently clobber each other.",
    "Snapshots plus an operation log make documents durable, and let you rebuild any version."
  ]
}
